  "Question": "Given \\(a^m=12\\) and \\(a^n=3\\) (\\(a\\ne0\\)), what is the value of \\(a^{m-n}\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The exponent domain matters here. Under the recorded interpretation \\(a>0\\) with real exponents \\(m,n\\), the same-base quotient law subtracts the exponents. Since \\(a^n=3\\ne0\\),\n\n\\(a^{m-n}=a^m\\div a^n=12\\div3=4\\).\n\nIf \\(m,n\\) are positive integers, raising the two givens to the powers \\(n\\) and \\(m\\), respectively, gives\n\\[\n12^n=(a^m)^n=(a^n)^m=3^m.\n\\]\nThe left side is even and the right side is odd, a contradiction. Thus the positive-integer interpretation has no solution. Real-exponent laws extend beyond the supplied Grade 8 scope, so the answer is conditional on the recorded real-power interpretation: \\(4\\).",
